# A TUF client learns every target name from the repository's own signed
# metadata: the targets.json it fetches and parses is a map whose keys are
# file names, and the delegated role names arrive the same way. tough's
# Repository::cache walks that map and, for each name, tough/src/cache.rs's
# cache_target fetches the target and writes it under
# `outdir.as_ref().join(filename)` through an OpenOptions write/create/open
# chain -- nothing normalises the name, checks the joined path stays inside
# the outdir, or encodes its path separators. The repository therefore
# decides the path components of a file written with the caching process's
# privileges: a target or delegated role named `../../.bashrc`-style walks
# the join out of the cache directory. CWE-22.
#
# The fixed revision introduces a TargetName type whose resolved() form path-cleans the
# raw name, and moves the storing half of cache_target into lib.rs's
# save_target, which canonicalises the outdir, joins the resolved name, and
# refuses to write unless the resulting file's parent directory starts_with
# the outdir. The delegated-role half of the change percent-encodes the name
# inside the delegated_filename/load_delegations callees.
#
# What fires without this change: nothing on the defect. The vulnerable tree
# reports 34 findings, and the only PATH rows are a taint finding on the
# caller-supplied metadata_outdir parameter and a VYQL-PATH-006 row on
# tuftool's download_root (present on both revisions, from the uri sibling
# below). cache_target's write is not even a bound sink -- OpenOptions open
# has no FilePathAccess binding -- and no Rust source concept can reach the
# name: it is deserialised out of serde_json inside schema parsing, a
# HashMap key several calls away from the join, so the taint route is out
# of reach in both directions.
#
# Nothing is minted. The archive-parsing neighbours in this family key the
# size-interpretation conflict (conditional vs absent extended-header
# override), which is not this shape; the weakness class -- a remote
# listing's names joined into local download paths -- is
# code.RemoteListingDownloadPath under VYQL-PATH-006. The addition is a
# sibling binding in vyql/bindings/rust/remote_uri_mirror_path_join.vyql
# for the case where the remote never hands the client a uri/url value:
# the names arrive inside a fetched and parsed document, and the function
# itself retrieves them by name -- a fetch/download-named call receiving
# the name's string form (as_str), a Path::join onto the local base, and
# the write through an OpenOptions builder chain or a plain create. The
# neutralising markers are std's containment vocabulary, exactly the uri
# sibling's, so the fixed revision's starts_with/canonicalize in save_target
# suppresses the row wherever it is written.
#
# Measured. The vulnerable revision reports cache_target
# (tough/src/cache.rs:206) as VYQL-PATH-006 and the patched revision
# reports no row there, three --cache off runs each; the discrimination
# rides on the join/write co-location and the std containment markers, not
# on any name the fixed revision introduces -- fetch_target, join, as_str and
# OpenOptions are identical in both revisions. Collateral, enumerated
# rather than sampled: the only other Rust trees under the sibling clones
# (thrift, tokio-tar and its patched copy) report zero PATH-006 rows, and
# grep-enumerating the shape's reachable set inside tough -- every file
# carrying both a join and a fetch/download call -- yields five files of
# which only cache_target matches; tuftool's download.rs joins CLI-supplied
# names with no fetch in the function, and lib.rs's load_delegations joins
# role names onto a Url for the fetch itself with no file write. The
# binding's residual, stated rather than hidden: the fetch and the join are
# co-located in one function but their arguments are not unified, so a
# function that fetches one remote name while storing a locally built path
# can still match -- the same residual the uri sibling carries for its
# join operand, and nothing in the measured trees exercises it. The
# delegated-role half of the changeed revision (percent-encoding inside
# delegated_filename) sanitises in the callee, so a function-local binding
# cannot see it; that is the known Rust delegated-control family, not a
# new gap.

test "rank1863: (a) a repository target name fetched by name and written under a join of itself, no containment"
  lang rust
  file tough/src/cache.rs
  expect VYQL-PATH-006
  code
  ```
  use std::fs::OpenOptions;
  use std::io::Read;
  use std::path::Path;

  pub struct Repository;
  pub struct Target;

  impl Repository {
      fn cache_target<P: AsRef<Path>>(&self, outdir: P, name: &str) -> Result<(), ()> {
          let t = self
              .targets
              .signed
              .find_target(name)
              .ok_or(())?;
          let (sha, filename) = self.target_digest_and_filename(t, name);
          let mut reader = self.fetch_target(t, &sha, filename.as_str())?;
          let path = outdir.as_ref().join(filename);
          let mut f = OpenOptions::new()
              .write(true)
              .create(true)
              .open(&path)?;
          std::io::copy(&mut reader, &mut f)?;
          Ok(())
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1863: (b) the patched save_target canonicalises the base and refuses a path outside it"
  lang rust
  file tough/src/lib.rs
  reject VYQL-PATH-006
  code
  ```
  use std::path::Path;

  pub struct Repository;

  impl Repository {
      pub fn save_target<P>(&self, name: &str, outdir: P) -> Result<(), ()>
      where
          P: AsRef<Path>,
      {
          let outdir = outdir.as_ref();
          let outdir = outdir.canonicalize()?;
          if !outdir.is_dir() {
              return Err(());
          }
          let filename = name.resolved();
          let resolved_filepath = outdir.join(filename);
          let filepath_dir = resolved_filepath.parent().ok_or(())?;
          if !filepath_dir.starts_with(&outdir) {
              return Err(());
          }
          let mut f = tempfile::NamedTempFile::new_in(&filepath_dir)?;
          f.persist(&resolved_filepath)?;
          Ok(())
      }
  }
  ```

